WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.320
Welcome to Module 5 of Global Secure
Access as the Identity-Aware Network:

2
00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:07.912
Internet Access and T L S inspection.

3
00:00:07.912 --> 00:00:12.587
Internet Access is the identity-aware
web gateway for remote users.

4
00:00:12.587 --> 00:00:16.329
T L S inspection comes last of
all, because it's the step most

5
00:00:16.329 --> 00:00:20.850
likely to break applications and to
read content the agency shouldn't.

6
00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:26.403
This module covers Steps 5 and 9 of the
Global Secure Access implementation guide,

7
00:00:26.403 --> 00:00:31.020
and takes about fifty minutes, plus
a lab in your own test tenant.

8
00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:35.920
Why does this matter?

9
00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:41.124
With Internet Access, Conditional Access
decides which security profile a user gets,

10
00:00:41.124 --> 00:00:43.982
and the profile decides
what the user can reach.

11
00:00:43.982 --> 00:00:47.756
It comes late in the run order
because it's separately licensed,

12
00:00:47.756 --> 00:00:52.619
and because an agency may keep its current
gateway; that's a decision from Module 1.

13
00:00:52.619 --> 00:00:55.158
T L S inspection comes last of all.

14
00:00:55.158 --> 00:00:57.919
It's the step most likely
to break applications,

15
00:00:57.919 --> 00:01:01.459
and the step most likely to read
content the agency shouldn't,

16
00:01:01.459 --> 00:01:05.070
so it starts only after filtering
works on server names alone.

17
00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:09.289
In this module you build a pilot
security profile, govern every bypass,

18
00:01:09.289 --> 00:01:13.217
and then add inspection from a
dedicated certificate authority,

19
00:01:13.217 --> 00:01:16.280
within a scope the
privacy officer approved.

20
00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:22.943
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module.

21
00:01:22.943 --> 00:01:26.015
You'll build a filtering
policy, a security profile,

22
00:01:26.015 --> 00:01:30.405
the link between them, and the
Conditional Access policy that assigns it.

23
00:01:30.405 --> 00:01:34.690
You'll explain why both the Microsoft
and internet profiles are enabled,

24
00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:36.618
and what Learn's limits leave out.

25
00:01:36.618 --> 00:01:40.854
You'll govern every bypass and
allow-list entry with an owner,

26
00:01:40.854 --> 00:01:42.380
a reason and an expiry.

27
00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:46.114
You'll choose the certificate
authority for T L S inspection,

28
00:01:46.114 --> 00:01:48.355
and deploy trust before activation.

29
00:01:48.355 --> 00:01:52.148
And you'll set the privacy boundary,
handle pinned applications,

30
00:01:52.148 --> 00:01:55.368
and read the T L S outcomes
against the one percent line.

31
00:01:55.368 --> 00:02:01.386
You need Module 2, Network and Transport,
chapter five, on Internet Access policy,

32
00:02:01.386 --> 00:02:07.130
and a skim of PKI and Certificates, chapter
four, for the dedicated issuing C A.

33
00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:10.660
The lab runs in a test
tenant, never production.

34
00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:18.623
Unit 1 is security profiles,
built with Learn's Graph tutorial.

35
00:02:18.623 --> 00:02:24.191
A filtering policy blocks the categories
the agency's acceptable-use policy names;

36
00:02:24.191 --> 00:02:29.598
artificial intelligence is Learn's example,
and the category list is the agency's.

37
00:02:29.598 --> 00:02:32.914
A security profile groups
web content filtering,

38
00:02:32.914 --> 00:02:36.635
threat intelligence, T L S
inspection and file policies.

39
00:02:36.635 --> 00:02:41.435
A policy link puts the filtering policy
into the profile, with logging on.

40
00:02:41.435 --> 00:02:44.533
A Conditional Access policy
assigns the profile:

41
00:02:44.533 --> 00:02:49.597
it targets All internet resources with
Global Secure Access, with the filtering

42
00:02:49.597 --> 00:02:54.660
profile session control, scoped to the
pilot, and with emergency access excluded.

43
00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:59.109
The baseline profile applies to all
traffic, last; the guide puts threat

44
00:02:59.109 --> 00:03:03.935
intelligence filtering there, so it
covers everyone in the internet profile.

45
00:03:03.935 --> 00:03:08.235
And enable both profiles: the
internet profile doesn't include the

46
00:03:08.235 --> 00:03:13.200
Microsoft traffic profile's destinations,
so full coverage needs both.

47
00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:17.788
Unit 2.

48
00:03:17.788 --> 00:03:24.291
First, Learn's limits: two hundred fifty-six
security profiles, a thousand policies,

49
00:03:24.291 --> 00:03:30.126
a thousand rules and eight thousand
destinations per tenant; H T T P and H T T

50
00:03:30.126 --> 00:03:35.674
P S on ports eighty and four forty-three
only; no I P version six, and no U D P.

51
00:03:35.674 --> 00:03:37.338
Second, QUIC.

52
00:03:37.338 --> 00:03:41.113
Internet Access doesn't
forward QUIC end to end.

53
00:03:41.113 --> 00:03:46.161
Learn's newer guidance blocks it with the
cloud firewall, which is preview; its T

54
00:03:46.161 --> 00:03:51.287
L S inspection page blocks outbound U D
P four forty-three with Windows Firewall.

55
00:03:51.287 --> 00:03:56.441
Until the cloud firewall for clients is
generally available, the guide uses the

56
00:03:56.441 --> 00:04:01.438
generally available path: a firewall
rule delivered by Intune, piloted first.

57
00:04:01.438 --> 00:04:06.329
Third, every custom bypass and allow-list
entry gets an owner's approval,

58
00:04:06.329 --> 00:04:11.801
a reason, and an expiry, in the exception
register, as Learn's change guidance asks.

59
00:04:11.801 --> 00:04:14.630
Custom bypass doesn't
work on remote networks;

60
00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:17.013
those are made on the branch equipment.

61
00:04:17.013 --> 00:04:19.929
And one gap: the pages
read don't say whether

62
00:04:19.929 --> 00:04:23.262
a report-only policy
applies a security profile.

63
00:04:23.262 --> 00:04:26.947
So the pilot is a policy set to
on for the pilot group only,

64
00:04:26.947 --> 00:04:31.480
with an allow policy, with logging,
for categories under review.

65
00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:37.942
Unit 3 is T L S inspection.

66
00:04:37.942 --> 00:04:41.798
It decrypts H T T P S at the
service edge, so full U R L's,

67
00:04:41.798 --> 00:04:47.440
files and content can be filtered, then
re-encrypts; it's the prerequisite

68
00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:52.518
for U R L rules, file and content
policies, and A I prompt inspection.

69
00:04:52.518 --> 00:04:56.617
Learn offers a Microsoft-managed
certificate, or the agency's own.

70
00:04:56.617 --> 00:05:00.395
With the agency's own, the agency
signs an intermediate C A,

71
00:05:00.395 --> 00:05:04.493
and the service issues short-lived
leaf certificates from it.

72
00:05:04.493 --> 00:05:08.890
Learn's limits: valid at least six
months, only one active at a time,

73
00:05:08.890 --> 00:05:13.205
with server authentication and
the C A extensions Learn lists.

74
00:05:13.205 --> 00:05:14.193
Which C A?

75
00:05:14.193 --> 00:05:19.143
Learn's tutorial suggests a dedicated
C A, not your production P K I.

76
00:05:19.143 --> 00:05:23.865
The program issues it from a dedicated
issuing C A under the agency's root,

77
00:05:23.865 --> 00:05:28.430
never from the C A that issues
authentication certificates, because a

78
00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:33.467
compromised inspection C A can impersonate
any site to every managed device.

79
00:05:33.467 --> 00:05:35.645
Deploy trust before activation:

80
00:05:35.645 --> 00:05:40.643
the root and intermediate go to clients
by Intune first, because an expired

81
00:05:40.643 --> 00:05:45.480
inspection certificate disables all
T L S inspection across the tenant.

82
00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:48.010
Renew at least ninety days ahead.

83
00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:53.785
Three more things before you inspect.

84
00:05:53.785 --> 00:05:58.628
The privacy scope: Learn auto-creates
a system bypass rule for incompatible

85
00:05:58.628 --> 00:06:03.010
destinations, and an editable
recommended bypass for the Education,

86
00:06:03.010 --> 00:06:06.623
Finance, Government, and Health
and Medicine categories.

87
00:06:06.623 --> 00:06:10.782
The guide keeps them, adds the
categories the privacy officer names,

88
00:06:10.782 --> 00:06:14.497
and records the scope in the
system's privacy documentation.

89
00:06:14.497 --> 00:06:17.611
Learn says to establish
and communicate a T

90
00:06:17.611 --> 00:06:20.810
L S policy to users
before inspection starts.

91
00:06:20.810 --> 00:06:23.298
Pinned applications fail under inspection;

92
00:06:23.298 --> 00:06:26.457
Learn's answer is a custom
bypass for their destinations,

93
00:06:26.457 --> 00:06:28.542
without duplicating the system list,

94
00:06:28.542 --> 00:06:32.710
and developer tools with their own
certificate stores need the root added.

95
00:06:32.710 --> 00:06:36.216
And the failure line:
Microsoft's recommendation treats

96
00:06:36.216 --> 00:06:39.572
a failure rate above one
percent as a systemic problem.

97
00:06:39.572 --> 00:06:47.572
Inspection doesn't negotiate H T T P two,
and doesn't follow A I A or O C S P links.

98
00:06:47.572 --> 00:06:51.836
Learn's pages give the T L S status
values differently in two places,

99
00:06:51.836 --> 00:06:56.410
so group by what your workspace
actually holds before writing an alert.

100
00:06:56.410 --> 00:06:57.773
Where are agencies?

101
00:06:57.773 --> 00:07:03.236
Internet Access and T L S inspection need
an Internet Access or Suite license,

102
00:07:03.236 --> 00:07:07.334
and are available in G C C by
Learn's product-wide statement.

103
00:07:07.334 --> 00:07:12.384
The cloud firewall for clients and the
Explicit Forward Proxy are preview,

104
00:07:12.384 --> 00:07:15.910
and their G C C availability
isn't yet specified.

105
00:07:19.660 --> 00:07:24.485
The lab runs in a test tenant, never
production, and the authors haven't run it.

106
00:07:24.485 --> 00:07:28.735
You need Module 2's lab done and
an Internet Access test license.

107
00:07:28.735 --> 00:07:32.847
Step one finds the internet profile
with Learn's filtered request,

108
00:07:32.847 --> 00:07:36.435
enables it, and assigns the
pilot group in the admin center.

109
00:07:36.435 --> 00:07:38.888
Step two creates the filtering policy,

110
00:07:38.888 --> 00:07:42.797
blocking Learn's example
category, artificial intelligence.

111
00:07:42.797 --> 00:07:45.870
Step three creates the
pilot security profile

112
00:07:45.870 --> 00:07:48.548
and links the policy to
it, with logging on.

113
00:07:48.548 --> 00:07:52.147
Step four assigns the profile
with Conditional Access,

114
00:07:52.147 --> 00:07:55.511
scoped to the pilot, with
emergency access excluded.

115
00:07:55.511 --> 00:07:59.308
Step five tests it: on the
pilot device, as a pilot user,

116
00:07:59.308 --> 00:08:03.547
browse to a site in that category
and expect the block page.

117
00:08:03.547 --> 00:08:06.281
Then the guide's traffic
query in the workspace

118
00:08:06.281 --> 00:08:09.450
should show a denied count
on the pilot user's row.

119
00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:18.703
Step six adds the QUIC rule on the pilot
device, Learn's Windows Firewall command;

120
00:08:18.703 --> 00:08:23.288
in production the same rule goes
out through Intune, piloted first.

121
00:08:23.288 --> 00:08:27.607
Steps seven and eight need a T L
S inspection certificate created,

122
00:08:27.607 --> 00:08:30.167
enabled and trusted on the test device,

123
00:08:30.167 --> 00:08:35.367
and a pilot T L S policy linked to the
pilot security profile, never the baseline,

124
00:08:35.367 --> 00:08:37.367
as the guide's Step 9 describes,

125
00:08:37.367 --> 00:08:40.887
with the privacy officer's
approval of the test scope.

126
00:08:40.887 --> 00:08:45.841
Step seven checks the issuer of an
inspected site in the browser's certificate

127
00:08:45.841 --> 00:08:50.413
viewer: Microsoft Global Secure Access
Intermediate, or your test chain.

128
00:08:50.413 --> 00:08:56.007
Step eight reads the T L S outcomes: T L
S action reads bypassed or intercepted,

129
00:08:56.007 --> 00:08:59.825
and the failure share should
stay under one percent.

130
00:08:59.825 --> 00:09:02.937
Step nine exports the
profiles and policies.

131
00:09:02.937 --> 00:09:04.288
Keep it all in place.

132
00:09:04.288 --> 00:09:08.247
Module 6 removes it, and the
T L S policy in Learn's order:

133
00:09:08.247 --> 00:09:13.280
the link from the profile, then
the policy, then the certificate.

134
00:09:17.030 --> 00:09:19.641
The federal requirement, from NIST special

135
00:09:19.641 --> 00:09:22.905
publication eight hundred
fifty-three, revision five.

136
00:09:22.905 --> 00:09:28.821
Internet Access supports S C seven, its
enhancement five, S I three and S I four:

137
00:09:28.821 --> 00:09:34.268
web access filtered by identity-aware
policy, with bypasses governed.

138
00:09:34.268 --> 00:09:39.249
The evidence is the filtering policy,
profile and Conditional Access exports,

139
00:09:39.249 --> 00:09:42.468
the blocked-category test,
and the bypass register.

140
00:09:42.468 --> 00:09:48.134
T L S inspection supports S C eight, S
C twelve, S C seventeen and S I four:

141
00:09:48.134 --> 00:09:51.777
encrypted web traffic inspected within an

142
00:09:51.777 --> 00:09:55.318
approved privacy scope,
with a governed C A.

143
00:09:55.318 --> 00:10:00.610
The evidence is the certificate and chain,
the trust profile's deployment status,

144
00:10:00.610 --> 00:10:04.006
the policy export, the issuer
check, the failure rate,

145
00:10:04.006 --> 00:10:06.692
the privacy approval and the user notice.

146
00:10:06.692 --> 00:10:11.814
The program's mapping lines Step 5 up with
tick three point oh's Remote User Pattern

147
00:10:11.814 --> 00:10:14.149
three, which CISA calls the riskiest,

148
00:10:14.149 --> 00:10:18.217
and Step 9 with tick's web policy
enforcement point capabilities.

149
00:10:18.217 --> 00:10:22.370
The evidence supports the
controls; the assessor decides.

150
00:10:26.120 --> 00:10:28.095
Check yourself with two questions.

151
00:10:28.095 --> 00:10:31.362
First: which certificate
authority issues the T L S

152
00:10:31.362 --> 00:10:34.707
inspection intermediate,
in the program's position?

153
00:10:34.707 --> 00:10:39.232
The answer is b: a dedicated
issuing C A under the agency's root,

154
00:10:39.232 --> 00:10:43.395
never the C A that issues
authentication certificates.

155
00:10:43.395 --> 00:10:47.257
Second: what happens when the
inspection certificate expires?

156
00:10:47.257 --> 00:10:51.299
The answer is b: all T L S
inspection across the tenant stops,

157
00:10:51.299 --> 00:10:57.108
so the guide renews at least ninety days
ahead and deploys trust before activation.

158
00:10:57.108 --> 00:11:02.370
The module page has three more questions,
each linked to the unit that answers it.

159
00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:06.945
To sum up.

160
00:11:06.945 --> 00:11:09.863
You enabled the internet
profile for the pilot,

161
00:11:09.863 --> 00:11:15.174
built a security profile with one blocked
category and assigned it with Conditional

162
00:11:15.174 --> 00:11:17.867
Access, proved the block,
added the QUIC rule,

163
00:11:17.867 --> 00:11:21.383
checked the inspection issuer,
and read the T L S outcomes.

164
00:11:21.383 --> 00:11:25.802
The evidence an I S S O keeps from
this module: the filtering profile,

165
00:11:25.802 --> 00:11:28.454
policy and Conditional Access exports;

166
00:11:28.454 --> 00:11:33.035
the blocked-category test and the
traffic query; the bypass register;

167
00:11:33.035 --> 00:11:38.098
the certificate and chain, the trust
profile's deployment status, the issuer

168
00:11:38.098 --> 00:11:42.920
check and the failure share; and the
privacy approval and the user notice.

169
00:11:42.920 --> 00:11:48.171
Next is Module 6, rings, rollback and the
evidence package, where the pilot becomes

170
00:11:48.171 --> 00:11:53.160
a migration, and where you roll the whole
test tenant back in the guide's order.
